package duckdb:extension@5.0.0;

// A host import for EXECUTING SQL from inside an extension callback -- the
// counterpart to `query`, which is READ-ONLY and gracefully degrades on
// re-entrancy. `nested-exec` runs the SQL on a SIBLING connection to the same
// underlying database, sidestepping both the core-mutex contention and the
// wasm-store re-entrancy that block `query` from inside an outer statement.
// That makes it usable from scalar/table callbacks -- the mechanism the
// workspace/notebook engine (`ducklink:fieldbook`) needs to execute a stored
// entry from `fieldbook_run(...)`.
//
// Kept as a SEPARATE interface (additive, like `query` / `collation` /
// `files-reg`) so existing components' frozen WIT copies stay structurally
// unchanged. Only exec-capable components (fieldbook) import it.
//
// TRUST MODEL: the sibling connection sees the same catalog + files as the
// outer connection but runs in a fresh, autocommitted transaction. A
// `fieldbook_run` invoked inside an outer `BEGIN` therefore sees the
// *committed* state, not the outer transaction's uncommitted changes. This is
// documented, not a bug -- the alternative (sharing the outer transaction)
// would re-introduce the very re-entrancy this interface exists to avoid.
//
// NESTING: the host enforces a nesting-depth ceiling (default 4). A component
// that would exceed it gets an error result. This prevents accidental
// cascades (a fieldbook entry that itself calls `fieldbook_run`) from
// unbounded recursion.
interface nested-exec {
  // Result of a nested execution. `rows` is present for row-producing
  // statements (SELECT / RETURNING / PRAGMA that returns rows); every cell is
  // stringified (NULL -> the empty string), matching the neutral row shape
  // used by the `query` interface. `rows-affected` is present for DML
  // (INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE) and reports the changed-row count.
  record exec-result {
    rows: option<list<list<string>>>,
    rows-affected: option<u64>,
  }

  // Run `sql` on a sibling connection to the same database. On success the
  // caller receives an `exec-result`; on failure it receives the SQL error
  // message verbatim (or a nesting-depth-exceeded error). Callers MUST NOT
  // trap on error -- treat it as a data failure and surface the message to
  // the user.
  nested-exec: func(sql: string) -> result<exec-result, string>;
}
